From: Jason Perlow <jperlow@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] thermal: cpufreq_cooling: don't error on missing OF node under ACPI

of_cpufreq_cooling_register() looks the CPU up with of_get_cpu_node() and,
when that returns NULL, logs at pr_err:

  cpufreq_cooling: OF node not available for cpu6

On Sky1 (O6N) the system boots via ACPI, so CPUs have no device-tree node
by design. The cpufreq core still calls this OF registration helper for
every policy, producing one error line per CPU at boot. The registration
correctly bails (returns NULL) — the message is a false alarm, not a real
failure.

Root cause: an OF-only cooling-register API invoked on an ACPI system,
where a missing CPU OF node is expected rather than a misconfiguration.

DEMOTED: the message is demoted to pr_debug when ACPI is active
(!acpi_disabled). On a genuine device-tree boot a missing CPU node is
still a real problem, so the original pr_err is preserved for the OF case
(acpi_disabled, which is a compile-time 1 when CONFIG_ACPI is off). No
functional change to cooling-device registration.

Signed-off-by: Jason Perlow <jperlow@gmail.com>
Upstream-Status: Pending
---
--- a/drivers/thermal/cpufreq_cooling.c
+++ b/drivers/thermal/cpufreq_cooling.c
@@ -10,6 +10,7 @@
  *		Viresh Kumar <viresh.kumar@linaro.org>
  *
  */
+#include <linux/acpi.h>
 #include <linux/cpu.h>
 #include <linux/cpufreq.h>
 #include <linux/cpu_cooling.h>
@@ -678,8 +679,18 @@
 	struct thermal_cooling_device *cdev = NULL;
 
 	if (!np) {
-		pr_err("cpufreq_cooling: OF node not available for cpu%d\n",
-		       policy->cpu);
+		/*
+		 * On ACPI systems CPUs legitimately have no OF node, so this
+		 * is an expected condition rather than an error. Only warn
+		 * loudly when device-tree is in use and the node is genuinely
+		 * missing; on ACPI demote it to a debug message.
+		 */
+		if (!acpi_disabled)
+			pr_debug("cpufreq_cooling: no OF node for cpu%d (ACPI)\n",
+				 policy->cpu);
+		else
+			pr_err("cpufreq_cooling: OF node not available for cpu%d\n",
+			       policy->cpu);
 		return NULL;
 	}
 
